From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: load the gaokun3 factory calibration into the 3D
 LUT

The factory calibration of the gaokun3 panel is a pair of 17x17x17 LUTs,
one for sRGB and one for Display P3. Reducing them to PCC matrices left
an average error of 5.6 dE for sRGB, against 1.1 for the LUT itself.

Resample both LUTs to the 13 points of the DSPP 3D LUT, which stays at
1.1 dE (0.2 dE from the 17-point LUT, panel model of one unit), and keep
them in its two banks. Each DSPP remembers whether its banks hold them;
a modeset that leaves the CRTC active clears that and writes them again,
as it may bring DSPPs that lost power.

The connector colorspace picks the bank per commit: BT709_YCC shows
sRGB, DCI-P3_RGB_D65 and the default colorspace show P3. A client that
leaves the colorspace alone therefore gets sRGB content stretched over
the panel's near-P3 gamut, with the calibrated white and primaries,
which is how Windows' P3 mode looks on this machine; it used to get the
sRGB calibration. Switching banks is a single opmode write, so P3
content coming and going does not tear.

PCC goes back to the CTM alone. It now runs after the calibration, so a
CTM such as Night Light acts on panel values. A CRTC still gets DSPPs
only once a CTM or gamma LUT is set, which the Android HWC always does;
a client that never sets either is not calibrated.
msm.panel_calibration=0 still turns the calibration off at runtime.

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
